Lexus is generally recognized for producing comfortable and dependable vehicles. For many drivers, purchasing a Lexus car feels like a safe, low-risk decision.

But even dependable brands have problem years.

At [CLINE APC](/), we’ve worked with plenty of Lexus lemon law cases over the years. And while newer Lexus models have generally improved, some older ones, especially those from 15 to 20 years ago, continue to appear in buyback claims.

Fortunately, California’s updated lemon law (as of 2025) still protects buyers of used vehicles in many situations. If an older Lexus car was sold with a qualifying warranty and keeps having the same problems, you may still have rights under the [Lexus car lemon law](/info/a-comprehensive-guide-to-california-lemon-law).

Below are five Lexus model years with a consistent pattern of owner complaints. In case you already own one, or you’re considering buying one under a dealer warranty, pay close attention to how these issues connect to new California lemon law changes in 2025.

## 1) 2007 Lexus ES 350 Problems

The [2007 Lexus ES 350](https://www.carcomplaints.com/Lexus/ES_350/2007/#google_vignette) remains one of the most complaint-heavy Lexus sedans on record. While many owners enjoy long-term reliability, this model year produced a wide range of recurring mechanical and interior problems. More than 800 complaints have been reported over time.

Common 2007 Lexus ES 350 problems include:

- Transmission malfunctions

- Engine oil leaks

- Steering and suspension problems

- Dashboard and door panel material deterioration

- Electrical and lighting failures

- Cooling fan breakdowns

- Premature brake wear

- Air conditioning and heater failures

- Power window and windshield defects

- Fuel system and drivetrain problems

These aren’t one-off complaints. Many 2007 Lexus ES 350 owners report the same defects returning even after repairs.

If your 2007 Lexus ES 350 is still under a dealer warranty, or you bought it recently and it’s now in and out of the shop, you may have a valid lemon law claim.

## 2) 2005 Lexus RX 330 Problems

The 2005 Lexus RX 330 helped put Lexus on the map in the luxury crossover category. The 2005 model year, however, produced a steady flow of complaints that continue to surface today.

Frequently reported 2005 Lexus RX 330 problems include:

- Transmission failures

- Headlight and lighting malfunctions

- Power steering fluid leaks

- Air conditioning breakdowns

- Water pump failures

- Engine and cooling system defects

- Brake system wear and failures

- Seat belt and airbag concerns

- Suspension and drivetrain issues

Experiencing any issues with your Lexus model warrants a trip to a technician. Get the vehicle inspected, keep copies of every repair order, and track how often the same issue comes back. This paperwork becomes critical if you later need to pursue lemon law for a used Lexus in California.

## 3) 2004 Lexus RX 330 Problems

The 2004 RX 330 sits in a similar category, though complaint volume is even higher. Owners reported problems across core systems, from drivetrains to interior materials.

Reported 2004 Lexus RX 330 problems include:

- Transmission failures

- Power steering leaks

- Dashboard cracking and melting

- Water pump and cooling system failures

- AC system breakdowns

- Brake wear well before expected intervals

- Window and windshield defects

- Suspension and wheel hub failures

- Fuel system and exhaust issues

For a vehicle that was once positioned as a luxury workhorse, this range of failures can turn ownership into a constant repair cycle. Some drivers report solving one issue, only to have another show up weeks later.

## 4) 2008 Lexus ES 350 Problems

The 2008 Lexus ES 350 is a midsize luxury sedan, not an SUV. Although it earned praise for ride quality, complaint patterns tell a different story under the hood.

Drivers have reported 2008 Lexus ES 350 problems across several core systems, including:

- Transmission failures

- Engine oil leaks

- Steering system problems

- Interior trim and panel deterioration

- Cooling fan breakdowns

- Traction control and ABS warning light failures

- AC and heater malfunctions

- Suspension defects

- Electrical and lighting issues

When these types of problems keep recurring, it’s more than just wear and tear. These patterns can point to underlying design flaws or reliability concerns that cost owners time, money, and peace of mind.

## 5) 2007 Lexus RX 350 Problems

The 2007 Lexus RX 350 is one of the most frequently cited Lexus car models in lemon law cases. On paper, it offers the smooth ride and quiet cabin Lexus is known for. But in practice, many owners have dealt with repeat mechanical issues that surface shortly after purchase, and persist even after multiple repair attempts.

Owners most often report 2007 Lexus RX 350 problems, such as:

- Transmission malfunctions

- Power steering leaks

- Water pump failures

- Air conditioning system breakdowns

- Electrical and lighting problems

- Suspension and drivetrain defects

- Brake wear far earlier than expected

These problems tend to recur across multiple vehicles, often despite maintenance or repair. Transmission complaints and steering leaks are especially common, and many drivers report the same issue returning within months of service.

## Lexus & Lemon Law in 2025: How the Rules Actually Apply

California’s lemon law protections remain some of the strongest in the U.S. But the procedures changed in 2025 with [AB 1755 lemon law](https://calmatters.digitaldemocracy.org/bills/ca_202320240ab1755?slug=CA_202320240AB1755).

Under AB 1755, the new procedures include:

- A mandatory written notice to the manufacturer before a lawsuit can move forward

- Shorter response timelines for manufacturers once notice is sent

- A structured pre-litigation process, including mediation, for opt-in manufacturers

- More rigid procedural deadlines that can bar claims if missed

- A system designed to reduce court filings by routing cases through early resolution steps

However, **those changes do not apply to Toyota or Lexus**.

## Why AB 1755 Doesn’t Apply to Lexus Vehicles

When the new law (AB 1755) passed, it created two paths for manufacturers:

- Opt in to the new process under [Senate Bill 26 (SB 26)](https://legiscan.com/CA/text/SB26/id/3153225), which includes stricter timelines, mediation, and mandatory notices.

- Stay under the original lemon law process (the Song-Beverly Act).

Toyota did not opt in. That means Lexus lemon law claims continue under the original Song-Beverly rules, not the updated AB 1755 process.

So you’re not required to send formal notice by certified mail. You don’t have to enter mandatory mediation. And your deadlines follow the traditional six-year rule, not the shorter ones introduced in AB 1755.

## Key Reminders for Lexus Lemon Law Claims in California

Even though AB 1755 doesn’t apply to Lexus vehicles, California’s original lemon law still gives you a path forward, as long as you follow the right steps and document everything carefully.

## You Still Need Proof

Even under the original rules, your claim needs evidence. Keep service invoices, inspection notes, and repair records. If the same issue happens more than once, you’ll want a paper trail.

## Used Cars Can Qualify

If your used Lexus car model came with a dealer-issued warranty (not just the remaining factory balance), lemon law may still apply. CPO cars sold through official Lexus dealers usually qualify as well.

## Leased Vehicles Count Too

Many people ask us, “Does lemon law apply to a leased Lexus in 2025?”

Yes, leased Lexus vehicles are covered under the California lemon law. You don’t need to be the buyer to qualify, as long as the vehicle is under warranty and the defects are real and repeatable.

## How to Protect Yourself When Buying a Lexus in 2025

Buying a Lexus may feel like a safe decision. And for many drivers, it is. However, even well-known brands can produce problem years, and when issues pop up, they’re rarely cheap. Here’s how to protect yourself from getting stuck with someone else’s problem.

## Always Get a Pre-Purchase Inspection

Even if the [car is certified pre-owned](/blog/cpo-cars-in-san-diego-lemon-law-buyer-tips) or comes from a Lexus dealership, have it inspected by a trusted independent mechanic before you sign anything. Certification doesn’t guarantee a defect-free car.

Trained technicians can spot early signs of issues like transmission hesitation, steering wear, or hybrid battery decline: things that may not trigger a warning light but will cost you later.

## Read the Warranty

Dealer warranties vary. Some cover key systems like the powertrain or hybrid components. Others don’t. Before you buy, ask for the warranty in writing and go through it line by line. Take note of the coverage start date, end date, mileage cap, and any exclusions. If it’s not covered, it won’t help you if problems arise.

## Keep Every Repair Record and Invoice

From the first oil change to the fifth service appointment for the same squeaky brake issue, document everything. Repair orders, inspection reports, dealer estimates, and email chains are your best protection in a lemon law claim.

Even “no problem found” notes on service visits help establish a timeline that shows the issue wasn’t fixed, despite your effort to get it resolved.

## Don’t Overlook Leased Lexus Vehicles

Many people ask us, “Does lemon law apply to a leased Lexus in 2025?”

Leases are now explicitly covered under California’s updated lemon law. This means you still have rights if a leased Lexus develops repeat defects, **but you’ll need to follow the same rules:**

- Keep service records

- Monitor repair attempts

- Send a written notice early

Don’t assume leasing means fewer protections. It means you need to be just as diligent about documentation.

