## Frequently Asked Questions

## What are the 2025 changes to California Lemon Law (AB 1755)?

AB 1755 requires consumers to send a written notice to the manufacturer before taking legal action. The notice must include your name, VIN, defect details, and a refund or replacement demand. Skipping this step can void lemon law protections.

## How does AB 1755 affect Lexus lemon claims?

AB 1755 applies the same lemon law standards to Lexus vehicles but adds a mandatory notice requirement. It covers new, used, and leased Lexus vehicles. Without proper written notice, even valid Lexus lemon claims can be dismissed.

## What Lexus models are lemons under the 2025 law?

Lexus models like the NX, RX, and GX commonly appear in lemon claims for hybrid system failures and transmission issues. Claims may qualify after four failed repair attempts or if the vehicle spends 30 or more days out of service.

## Does California Lemon Law cover leased Lexus vehicles?

Yes. California Lemon Law explicitly covers leased Lexus vehicles when warranty defects persist. Lessees have the same protections as buyers, provided they document repairs and follow the AB 1755 notice requirements.

## How long do I have to file a lemon law claim in California in 2025?

The 18-month / 18,000-mile window is a lemon-law presumption, not a requirement. It does not decide whether you have a good case. What matters is whether the defect first occurred during the original manufacturer warranty. Written notice under AB 1755 should be sent as soon as repeat defects appear to avoid timing issues.

## What if my Lexus has repeated defects after the 2025 changes?

Start by documenting every repair visit and keeping copies of service records. Send the required AB 1755 notice promptly, then consult a California lemon law attorney like CLINE APC to review your options.
